{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** Fatty acid oxidation, also called beta-oxidation, breaks a fatty acid down two carbons at a time into acetyl CoA. It happens in the mitochondrial matrix and needs oxygen. Each cycle makes one FADH2 and one NADH. Long-chain fatty acids enter the mitochondria through the carnitine shuttle, which malonyl CoA inhibits. Complete oxidation of palmitate gives a net 129 ATP by older values.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="BC4.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is fatty acid oxidation?

Cells can use lipids as fuel as well as sugars. The pathway that does this is **fatty acid oxidation**, also called **beta-oxidation**. The long carbon chain of a fatty acid is cut two carbons at a time into acetyl groups, which are attached to coenzyme A (CoA) to form **acetyl CoA**. Acetyl CoA then enters the citric acid (TCA) cycle.

The name comes from the site of the cut. Two carbons at a time are removed starting from the carboxyl end, and the chain is broken between the alpha (carbon 2) and the beta (carbon 3) carbon atoms. So palmitoyl CoA (16 carbons) forms eight acetyl CoA molecules.

Key facts to hold on to:

- When the aim is to make ATP, the process takes place in the **mitochondria**. Peroxisomes can also carry out beta-oxidation, but that route is for special cases (see below).
- It is an **aerobic** process, so it needs oxygen.
- Each step involves acyl CoA derivatives, uses separate enzymes, and uses **NAD+ and FAD** as coenzymes.
- Fatty acids are oxidised by most tissues. The **brain, red blood cells (erythrocytes) and adrenal medulla** cannot use fatty acids for energy.
- Fatty acid oxidation in the mitochondria is kept separate from fatty acid synthesis in the cytosol. This lets each pathway be controlled on its own and fitted to the needs of the tissue.

## Where do the fatty acids come from?

When the body needs fatty acids for energy, the enzyme **hormone-sensitive lipase** in fat cells (adipocytes) starts the breakdown of stored triacylglycerol. It is activated by epinephrine and glucagon and inhibited by insulin. The released fatty acids are carried by serum albumin to the liver and other tissues.

In plasma, longer-chain free fatty acids are bound to **albumin**. Inside a cell they are attached to a **fatty acid-binding protein**, so they are never really free. Shorter-chain fatty acids are more water soluble.

## The three stages

Fatty acid oxidation has three stages:

1. **Activation** of the fatty acid to its acyl CoA derivative.
2. **Transport** of the activated fatty acid into the mitochondria.
3. **Beta-oxidation proper** in the mitochondrial matrix.

### Stage 1: activation

Fatty acids are activated to acyl CoA by enzymes called **thiokinases (acyl CoA synthetases)**. The reaction needs ATP, coenzyme A and magnesium ions and goes in two steps:

- The fatty acid reacts with ATP to form **acyladenylate**.
- Acyladenylate combines with coenzyme A to give **acyl CoA**.

In this process ATP is converted to pyrophosphate (PPi), so **two high-energy phosphates are used**. The enzyme inorganic pyrophosphatase breaks PPi down to phosphate at once. Removing PPi straight away makes the reaction **irreversible**. This is why activation of palmitate is counted as costing the equivalent of 2 ATP.

There are different thiokinases for different chain lengths: one for long-chain (10 to 20 carbon), one for medium-chain (4 to 12 carbon) and one for short-chain (fewer than 4 carbon) fatty acids.

### Stage 2: the carnitine shuttle

The inner mitochondrial membrane cannot be crossed by CoA. A long-chain acyl group therefore needs a carrier to move from the cytosol into the matrix. The carrier is **carnitine**, and this rate-limiting transport is called the **carnitine shuttle**.

{% table %}
- **Step**
- **Enzyme or carrier**
- **What happens**
---
- **1**
- **Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-I)**, on the outer part of the mitochondrial membrane system. Also called carnitine acyltransferase I.
- The acyl group moves from CoA to carnitine, forming **acylcarnitine** and releasing free CoA
---
- **2**
- **Carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase**
- Acylcarnitine is carried into the matrix in exchange for free carnitine
---
- **3**
- **Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT-II)**, on the inside of the inner membrane
- The acyl group moves from carnitine back to CoA in the matrix, re-forming acyl CoA and freeing carnitine
{% /table %}

Once inside, the acyl CoA is ready for beta-oxidation by matrix enzymes.

**The control point:** **malonyl CoA inhibits CPT-I.** Malonyl CoA is made when fatty acid synthesis is going on in the cytosol. Its presence blocks long-chain acyl groups from entering the matrix, so newly made palmitate cannot be sent straight into the mitochondria to be burned. This is how synthesis and oxidation avoid running at the same time.

**Medium-chain fatty acids are different.** They are plentiful in human milk, and their oxidation does not depend on CPT-I. So it is not blocked by malonyl CoA.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Carnitine shuttle in order:** CPT-I puts the acyl group **on** carnitine, the translocase carries it **in**, and CPT-II takes it **off** inside the matrix. Malonyl CoA switches off CPT-I.
{% /callout %}

## Stage 3: the four reactions of beta-oxidation

A group of enzymes, together called "fatty acid oxidase", sit in the mitochondrial matrix or the inner membrane next to the respiratory chain. They work in a cycle. One turn of the cycle is a sequence of four reactions around the beta carbon (carbon 3), and it shortens the fatty acid by two carbons.

{% table %}
- **Reaction**
- **Enzyme**
- **What happens**
---
- **1. Oxidation (dehydrogenation)**
- Acyl CoA dehydrogenase, which needs **FAD**
- Two hydrogen atoms are removed from carbons 2 and 3. This gives a trans-enoyl CoA and **FADH2**
---
- **2. Hydration**
- Enoyl CoA hydratase
- Water is added to the double bond to give 3-hydroxyacyl CoA
---
- **3. Second oxidation**
- L-3-hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase, which needs **NAD+**
- The hydroxyl group on carbon 3 is oxidised to a keto group, giving 3-ketoacyl CoA and **NADH**
---
- **4. Thiolytic cleavage**
- Thiolase (3-ketoacyl CoA thiolase)
- The 3-ketoacyl CoA is split between carbons 2 and 3. This releases **acetyl CoA** and an acyl CoA that is two carbons shorter
{% /table %}

The shorter acyl CoA goes back to reaction 1 and goes round again. In this way a long-chain fatty acid with an even number of carbons can be broken down completely into acetyl CoA (two-carbon units). When only a four-carbon acyl group is left, the last cleavage gives two acetyl CoA molecules.

In mitochondria there are four acyl CoA dehydrogenases, each with a preference for short-, medium-, long- or very-long-chain fatty acids. Every step of the cycle uses enzymes with chain-length specificity.

### How many cycles?

For a saturated fatty acid with an even number of carbons (n carbons), the four steps repeat **(n/2) minus 1 times**. Each cycle gives one acetyl group, one NADH and one FADH2. The final cleavage gives two acetyl groups.

For palmitoyl CoA (16 carbons):

- **7 cycles** of beta-oxidation
- **8 acetyl CoA**
- **7 FADH2**
- **7 NADH**

The overall reaction is: palmitoyl CoA + 7 CoASH + 7 FAD + 7 NAD+ + 7 H2O gives 8 acetyl CoA + 7 FADH2 + 7 NADH + 7 H+.

## Where does the energy go?

The FADH2 and NADH from each cycle pass to the electron transport chain and make ATP by oxidative phosphorylation. The acetyl CoA enters the citric acid cycle, combines with oxaloacetate to form citrate and is broken down to carbon dioxide and hydrogen atoms. This final breakdown is exactly the same as for the acetyl CoA made from pyruvate during glucose metabolism. The citric acid cycle is the final common pathway for the oxidation of all foodstuffs.

### ATP yield of palmitate

Complete oxidation of palmitoyl CoA to carbon dioxide and water gives 8 acetyl CoA, 7 NADH and 7 FADH2. By the older values:

{% table %}
- **Source**
- **ATP**
---
- 7 NADH, 3 ATP each
- 21
---
- 7 FADH2, 2 ATP each
- 14
---
- 8 acetyl CoA, 12 ATP each through the TCA cycle
- 96
---
- **Total**
- **131**
---
- Activation of palmitate (2 ATP equivalents)
- minus 2
---
- **Net yield**
- **129 ATP**
{% /table %}

Newer values give about 2.5 ATP per NADH and about 1.5 ATP per FADH2. On this basis the net yield of beta-oxidation plus the citric acid cycle for 1 mol of palmitate is **106 mol of ATP**. Know which set of values your department uses, and write the numbers with the values you state.

The standard free energy of palmitate is 2,340 Cal. The energy captured as 129 ATP is about 940 Cal, so the efficiency of energy conservation is about **40 percent**.

Unsaturated fatty acids give less energy than saturated ones. They are less highly reduced, so fewer reducing equivalents can be made from them.

## Vitamins and coenzymes needed

Fatty acid oxidation, and the citric acid cycle that follows it, depend on B vitamins:

- **Riboflavin** as FAD: used by acyl CoA dehydrogenase in beta-oxidation, and by succinate dehydrogenase in the TCA cycle
- **Niacin** as NAD+: used by 3-hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase, and as an electron acceptor in the TCA cycle
- **Pantothenic acid** as part of coenzyme A: carries the acyl groups and forms acetyl CoA and succinyl CoA
- **Thiamin** as thiamin diphosphate: a coenzyme for the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase step of the TCA cycle

## Special cases

### Odd-chain fatty acids

A saturated fatty acid with an odd number of carbons goes through the same reactions as an even-chain one until the last three carbons are left. This three-carbon piece is **propionyl CoA**. Propionyl CoA is also made in the breakdown of certain amino acids. It is handled in three steps:

1. **Propionyl CoA carboxylase** adds a carboxyl group to give D-methylmalonyl CoA. It needs **biotin**.
2. **Methylmalonyl CoA racemase** converts the D-form to L-methylmalonyl CoA.
3. **Methylmalonyl CoA mutase** rearranges the carbons to give **succinyl CoA**, which enters the TCA cycle. It needs the coenzyme form of **vitamin B12** (deoxyadenosylcobalamin).

Succinyl CoA from this pathway is the **only glucogenic precursor made from fatty acid oxidation**. The mutase reaction is one of only two reactions in the body that need vitamin B12.

In vitamin B12 deficiency, both propionate and methylmalonate are passed in the urine. Methylmalonic acidemia and aciduria can be inherited in two forms: one where the mutase is missing or deficient (or has reduced affinity for its coenzyme), and one where the patient cannot convert vitamin B12 into its coenzyme form. Either type causes metabolic acidosis, and some patients show developmental retardation.

### Unsaturated fatty acids

Unsaturated fatty acids need extra enzymes because their double bonds are in the wrong position or form for the normal enzymes.

- A monounsaturated fatty acid such as oleic acid needs one extra enzyme, **3,2-enoyl CoA isomerase**. It changes the double bond left at position 3 after three rounds of beta-oxidation into the 2-trans form that enoyl CoA hydratase needs.
- A polyunsaturated fatty acid such as linoleic acid also needs an **NADPH-dependent 2,4-dienoyl CoA reductase**, as well as the isomerase.

### Very-long-chain fatty acids and peroxisomes

Very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA), those 22 carbons long or longer, first go through a preliminary beta-oxidation in **peroxisomes**. The shortened fatty acid then goes to a mitochondrion for further oxidation.

The first step in peroxisomes uses an **FAD-containing acyl CoA oxidase**. The FADH2 formed is oxidised directly by molecular oxygen, which becomes hydrogen peroxide (H2O2). So **no ATP is made by this step**. The hydrogen peroxide is then reduced to water.

## Acetyl CoA and ketone bodies

Acetyl CoA from beta-oxidation has two possible fates. It can be oxidised in the citric acid cycle, or it can go into **ketogenesis** to form ketone bodies.

The term **ketone bodies** means three substances: **beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate and acetone**. In the liver:

- **HMG-CoA synthase** (the rate-limiting enzyme in mitochondria) converts acetyl CoA to hydroxymethylglutaryl (HMG) CoA.
- **HMG-CoA lyase** converts this to **acetoacetate**.
- Acetoacetate is either reduced to **beta-hydroxybutyrate** or converted to **acetone**.

**Only liver mitochondria can form ketone bodies.** Ketogenesis is necessary for life during nutritional deprivation. It spares glucose for the brain and other organs.

When carbohydrate is extremely low, as in fasting or starvation, the body switches to ketone bodies as fuel. During starvation gluconeogenesis uses up oxaloacetate, so acetyl CoA cannot enter the TCA cycle easily and is used to make ketone bodies instead. By the fourth day of fasting, the brain gets about 70 percent of its energy from ketone bodies.

As the level of serum free fatty acids rises, more of the acetyl CoA is turned into ketone bodies and less is oxidised to carbon dioxide. This split is regulated so that the total energy captured as ATP from fatty acid oxidation stays constant. Complete oxidation of 1 mol of palmitate gives 106 mol of ATP, but only 26 mol when acetoacetate is the end product and only 16 mol when 3-hydroxybutyrate is the end product. So ketogenesis lets the liver oxidise more and more fatty acid within the limits of a tightly coupled system of oxidative phosphorylation.

Extreme production of ketones happens in insulin deficiency, usually from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, and is a threat to life. Low insulin and high glucagon promote lipolysis, raise plasma free fatty acids and lead to ketoacidosis.

## Regulation

{% table %}
- **Point of control**
- **How it works**
---
- **Entry into mitochondria**
- The carnitine shuttle is rate-limiting. Malonyl CoA inhibits CPT-I
---
- **Release of fatty acids from fat**
- Hormone-sensitive lipase is activated by epinephrine and glucagon and inhibited by insulin
---
- **Link to gluconeogenesis**
- Acetyl CoA is a positive allosteric effector of pyruvate carboxylase, linking fatty acid oxidation and gluconeogenesis
---
- **Ketogenesis**
- Regulated at three steps, including the supply of oxaloacetate. A fall in oxaloacetate in the mitochondria reduces the ability of the citric acid cycle to handle acetyl CoA
{% /table %}

## Clinical correlation

### Fatty acid oxidation disorders

These inherited disorders mainly affect tissues with a high beta-oxidation flux: the liver, skeletal muscle and cardiac muscle. The most common presentation is an acute episode of life-threatening coma, hepatic encephalopathy and **hypoglycemia** brought on by fasting, because the liver cannot make ketone bodies normally. Other features can include chronic cardiomyopathy, muscle weakness and exercise-induced rhabdomyolysis.

The patient can seem well between episodes, when there is no fasting stress or increased energy demand. An acute presentation may be misdiagnosed as Reye syndrome, or, if fatal, as sudden unexpected infant death. These disorders are easy to miss because the only specific clue may be an inappropriately low level of ketones during the episode.

### MCAD deficiency

**Medium-chain acyl CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency** is an autosomal recessive disorder. It is the most common inborn error of fatty acid oxidation and one of the most common inborn errors of metabolism, found in 1 in 14,000 births worldwide, with a higher rate in Northern Europeans.

- The ability to oxidise fatty acids with **six to ten carbons** falls. They build up and can be measured in urine.
- **Severe hypoglycemia** follows, because the tissues must rely more on glucose.
- **Treatment** includes avoiding fasting.
- Some cases first reported as sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) or Reye syndrome were later found to be caused by MCAD deficiency.

## Mnemonics and summary lines

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Four steps of beta-oxidation:** Dehydrogenation (FAD), Hydration, Dehydrogenation (NAD+), Thiolysis. In the same order as the cycle, and the first two letters of each hint at the enzyme.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Cycle count:** for n carbons, (n/2) minus 1 cycles. Palmitate: 16 carbons, 7 cycles, 8 acetyl CoA.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The long-chain acyl group needs carnitine to cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, which CoA cannot cross. This rate-limiting transport is the carnitine shuttle." %}
Long-chain fatty acids are carried from the cytosol into the mitochondrial matrix by:
- A. Albumin
- B. Acyl carrier protein
- C. Carnitine
- D. Citrate
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Malonyl CoA, made during fatty acid synthesis in the cytosol, inhibits CPT-I. This stops newly made palmitate from entering the mitochondria to be oxidised." %}
Which compound inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I?
- A. Acetyl CoA
- B. Malonyl CoA
- C. Propionyl CoA
- D. Succinyl CoA
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="For an even-chain saturated fatty acid with n carbons, the four-step cycle repeats (n/2) minus 1 times. For palmitate (16 carbons) that is 7 cycles, giving 8 acetyl CoA." %}
How many cycles of beta-oxidation are needed to break palmitoyl CoA completely into acetyl CoA?
- A. 5
- B. 6
- C. 8
- D. 7
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Complete oxidation of palmitoyl CoA gives 131 ATP by the older values. Activation of the fatty acid uses the equivalent of 2 ATP, so the net yield is 129 ATP." %}
By the older values, the net ATP yield from complete oxidation of one molecule of palmitate is:
- A. 129
- B. 131
- C. 106
- D. 96
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Propionyl CoA is converted to succinyl CoA by carboxylation (biotin), racemisation, and a mutase reaction that needs vitamin B12. Succinyl CoA is the only glucogenic precursor made from fatty acid oxidation." %}
The three-carbon propionyl CoA formed from odd-chain fatty acids is converted to succinyl CoA. Which vitamin is needed for the mutase step?
- A. Biotin
- B. Vitamin B12
- C. Thiamin
- D. Riboflavin
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="MCAD deficiency reduces the ability to oxidise fatty acids with six to ten carbons, which build up in urine. Tissues rely more on glucose, so severe hypoglycemia follows, and fasting must be avoided." %}
A child has severe hypoglycemia after a period of fasting, and fatty acids with six to ten carbons are found in the urine. The most likely diagnosis is:
- A. Methylmalonic acidemia
- B. Vitamin B12 deficiency
- C. MCAD deficiency
- D. Insulin deficiency
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is beta-oxidation and where does it take place?" %}
It is the breakdown of fatty acids two carbons at a time into acetyl CoA, cutting between the alpha and beta carbons. For ATP production it takes place in the mitochondrial matrix. It is aerobic.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does fatty acid activation cost 2 ATP?" %}
ATP is converted to pyrophosphate (PPi) in the reaction, so two high-energy phosphates are used. Pyrophosphatase removes PPi at once, which makes the reaction irreversible.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the carnitine shuttle." %}
CPT-I transfers the acyl group from CoA to carnitine, forming acylcarnitine. The translocase carries acylcarnitine into the matrix in exchange for free carnitine. CPT-II transfers the acyl group back to CoA inside the matrix. It is the rate-limiting step, and malonyl CoA inhibits CPT-I.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the four reactions of beta-oxidation." %}
Oxidation by acyl CoA dehydrogenase (FAD, gives FADH2), hydration by enoyl CoA hydratase, a second oxidation by 3-hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase (NAD+, gives NADH), and thiolytic cleavage by thiolase to release acetyl CoA.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What does one molecule of palmitoyl CoA yield by beta-oxidation?" %}
Seven cycles give 8 acetyl CoA, 7 NADH and 7 FADH2. The net yield is 129 ATP by older values, after subtracting 2 ATP for activation.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does the oxidation of an odd-chain fatty acid matter in glucose metabolism?" %}
The last three carbons form propionyl CoA, which is converted to succinyl CoA. This is the only glucogenic precursor made from fatty acid oxidation.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which tissues cannot use fatty acids, and what do they use instead?" %}
The brain, erythrocytes and adrenal medulla cannot use fatty acids for energy. In prolonged fasting the brain switches to ketone bodies and by the fourth day gets about 70 percent of its energy from them.
{% /viva %}

